#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532763} :activity/id #uuid "0dddcfd5-0403-586e-b566-77d6cec7da24" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050264 :topic/id #uuid "3ab583b8-5390-5f48-b28b-1a89db70664f" :topic/math-academy-id 2152} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268875 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aeff67f-bc02-547f-b69b-6bdf349d6bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266664} {:answer/id #uuid "464dd515-028f-5aae-9e77-ae9b54d5fc52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266771} {:answer/id #uuid "6b764dd1-dfe8-50aa-a5fd-d1db95a386dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267094} {:answer/id #uuid "96d36511-74ca-540a-8f13-e1c443357c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267490} {:answer/id #uuid "f37ad1c1-6c5d-599c-8ad9-827f4e88e8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268309}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266664} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5ff1f2b-285a-59da-b8d8-4f3c9f57a37c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "731d6d86-2944-5a6b-a46c-4a8ce3596432" :question/math-academy-id "q-132562" :question/problem "![](images/d5/d5f54020da277980a58151bf2a85911365e67ecae6832881e03ac8b9b6431e6a.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}4x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} & =\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/d8/d8b8b05e9004f6b25f635e9c39421a8e8e27831a26a22273cf88572df89ffc45.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}4x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,2x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2{[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]}_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2[4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})]dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,[3-\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{1}{16}{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})dy & =6{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{48}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{64}{48}) \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7ffaedb1-3cdb-5281-bb22-78d7084be98d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532749 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32b79e3c-7cd3-5487-bf51-0cc7471f163a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266590} {:answer/id #uuid "5ef2b1e1-3369-5b83-9971-a39f5acd5a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266982} {:answer/id #uuid "613c72a6-55ca-5664-a897-a062042e4b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267003} {:answer/id #uuid "976e1c89-099f-5500-897d-a24955d254ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{2-{y}^{2}}^{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267498} {:answer/id #uuid "d707306f-643e-5f1c-a471-fefa9981716a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266982} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d39062-49c4-533f-8558-75a5341cb3ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "87601be3-b1cb-5be0-9f64-84da32d08da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132510" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f390e83f737b3b20931df5e97a9ea0750b8b190acba14d1cd963aaab5175ba50.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x=\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2 & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)+{y}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+2{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =0 \\\\ (3y+2)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$,\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/1b/1b67c65c78dc847c2bf4bf53ad67f770a1406570548497edd55939c3a68a4c5c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b56552d2-ac9f-592d-811a-c1c5df0b08de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532745}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532694 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ac4dd8-a2cc-5684-a714-755b28cbde6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531942} {:answer/id #uuid "07bcc752-37cb-578c-ba6f-1183cd21127e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531950} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5daeb0-21a3-5d1c-a7ed-985fc4522bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531995} {:answer/id #uuid "6d32a471-8c9b-5b78-b525-91d9afea5a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532181} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1ba99b-1908-5e85-9667-82240fdb0580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532186}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531950} :answer-field/id #uuid "c41d697e-2a49-505c-badb-e497a93f6ef5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b10f5b9b-b4a2-5c9d-902a-bb3a93461daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-81321" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{4}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{4}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}{y}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}}{y}^{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{3}\\,dy & ={[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{\\pi /4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d0559c23-f8a0-5319-b515-e438dd5d1c54" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532768}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d41a288-d75b-5411-b221-253c093a464e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{-y+2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324335} {:answer/id #uuid "48eeb512-17d7-5711-9c97-bc71a8e7c9d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y+2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324564} {:answer/id #uuid "b1fcdcbc-5523-5581-8d95-0338d8446350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y+2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325492} {:answer/id #uuid "bfcaaed7-bc6e-5086-943f-a2e18d2f3371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y-2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325621} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2afd68-f90a-5eb7-ba16-acdd6e4a6399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{-y+2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325771}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325492} :answer-field/id #uuid "680c9e14-d56f-505e-8f99-d54704206319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3786eeaa-4a33-58cb-9504-23515a98f21e" :question/math-academy-id "q-40581" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x-2,$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ above the $x$-axis. What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/68/6817e13f2f4c019f337425bd9f2284386c7077c0ac97da51fc26fdea19f8ee67.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x-2$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=y+2 \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =y+2 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](images/db/db9d1846b19b75a629ad0a0d4b6e138aa2cef91cd8c0324a9285ed9c44befb60.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le y+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y+2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eb5c893e-568a-5f00-90ec-65a62aa10b90" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532768 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cf7f686-e091-52dc-82fd-b94658d3ddbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266391} {:answer/id #uuid "2cb30592-4046-557a-ad5e-c601b382f307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266541} {:answer/id #uuid "7c93c473-65f5-55a3-9686-1031d94bc4b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267246} {:answer/id #uuid "92951d1f-7e54-5e7e-8d8e-4759b9ef6f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267451} {:answer/id #uuid "ed753ef8-6481-5146-a7c3-7a0b4d42c12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267451} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c92017b-41b6-5ac4-92b6-462024ebfd32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a5227312-f350-56e0-9d23-74214d0c95e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-132421" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fd2527f9-fa7c-5c78-a80a-ae376966b8cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532749}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Over Type II Regions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531929}]}
